## Test Smell Audit — Academic 19-Smell Catalog (testsmells.org)

**Target:** `DataAccess.IntegrationTests/UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`  
**Framework:** MSTest 4.1 (.NET 10) | **Calibration:** Integration (`[TestCategory("Integration")]`)

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 1              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### ❶ Conditional Test Logic — **High**

**Method:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 101)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Problem:** The `if/else` means one branch never executes — you're silently testing different things depending on runtime type. If the code ever returns a `PremiumUser` unexpectedly, the name assertion is skipped.

**Fix:** Split into two explicit tests with deterministic setup:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", 0.1m));
    var loaded = (PremiumUser)_repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com")!;
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — the test always "passes" but only one path is ever verified.

---

#### ❷ Sleepy Test — **High**

**Method:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);
```

**Problem:** Hard-coded 3-second sleep makes the suite slow and non-deterministic. On a slow CI agent the notification may take longer; on a fast machine you waste 3 seconds per run.

**Integration calibration note:** Even for integration tests, blocking sleeps are a smell (per Camara et al. — correlated with flakiness). Downgraded from *Critical* to *High* since timing coordination is inherent to integration scenarios, but a poll/wait pattern is still superior.

**Fix:** Use a polling helper with timeout:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var sent = await WaitUntilAsync(
        () => _repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

    Assert.IsTrue(sent, "Notification was not sent within timeout.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky failures in CI; 3 s of dead time per run; will get worse as more similar tests are added.

---

#### ❸ Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test) — **High**

**Method:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113)

```csharp
for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
{
    _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
}
// No assertions
```

**Problem:** This passes even if every insert silently fails (e.g., swallowed exceptions, no-op stub). The method name implies "no errors" but the framework only checks that no *exception* is thrown — it cannot detect logical failures.

**Fix:** Assert the observable outcome:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count, "All bulk-inserted users should be persisted.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — coverage looks good, but zero behaviors are verified.

---

#### ❹ Assertion Roulette — **Medium**

**Method:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35-38)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

**Problem:** Four assertions with no message parameters. In MSTest's test runner output, a failure reports only `Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<null>.` — with no hint about *which property* was wrong without opening the source.

**Fix:** Add discriminating messages:

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert.");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip.");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip.");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Id should be assigned by the database.");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slow diagnosis in CI logs; developers must open source to understand which property failed.

---

### Smell-Free Patterns ✓

The following tests are well-designed — clear arrange/act/assert, single behavior, deterministic, with appropriate assertions:

| Test | Why it's good |
|------|---------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Single behavior, focused assertions, hermetic setup |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Clean negative assertion (`Assert.IsNull`) |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count is self-documenting (3 items added → assert 3) |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Exercises real concurrency with a verifiable outcome |

The use of **in-memory SQLite** (`Data Source=:memory:`) is excellent — it provides real database semantics without Mystery Guest coupling to external infrastructure.

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Effort | Action |
|----------|-------|--------|--------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | 5 min | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, ...)` to `BulkInsert` |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | 10 min | Split `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` into two tests |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | 15 min | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with poll/timeout utility |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | 5 min | Add message strings to `InsertUser` assertions |

**Bottom line:** 4 of 8 tests have catalog smells. The high-severity trio (#1–#3) all risk **silent false-passes** — they should be fixed before your next release. The Assertion Roulette (#4) is quality-of-life and can be addressed during normal maintenance.
